If you are picking an agent SDK in late 2026, you are really choosing between three different ideas of what an "agent framework" is. The Claude Agent SDK hands you the same agent loop, tools and permission system that power Claude Code, packaged as a library. The OpenAI Agents SDK is a small set of primitives (agents, tools, handoffs, guardrails, sessions) with tracing built in, and since April 2026 a sandbox layer for agents that work on real files. Google's Agent Development Kit (ADK) is the broadest of the three: five languages, graph-based workflows in the 2.0 line, and deep hooks into Google Cloud.

The short version, as of November 27, 2026: pick the Claude Agent SDK if you want a ready-made coding or computer-using agent with files, shell, hooks and skills out of the box. Pick the OpenAI Agents SDK if you want the lightest orchestration layer with first-party tracing and a choice of sandbox providers. Pick Google ADK if you need deterministic workflows, Java, Go or Kotlin, or you already deploy on Google Cloud. All three are free to install; you pay for model tokens. This post also covers how each one handles agent skills (SKILL.md folders), because that is where they differ most, and five alternatives (LangGraph, Vercel AI SDK, Mastra, Microsoft Agent Framework and Strands Agents).

{% youtube id="tK32trvj_b4" title="Build Hour: Agents SDK" /%}

*OpenAI's "Build Hour: Agents SDK" video. We confirmed it exists and its title via YouTube oEmbed on November 27, 2026; we did not watch it or check its upload date, so APIs and model names in it may be older than the docs quoted below.*

## Short answer and quick picks

- **Best for a ready-made, Claude Code-style agent: Claude Agent SDK.** Built-in file, shell and search tools, hooks, subagents, sessions and filesystem skills, in Python and TypeScript.
- **Best for light orchestration plus tracing: OpenAI Agents SDK.** Handoffs, guardrails, sessions and a default-on trace dashboard, with optional sandbox agents on seven-plus providers.
- **Best for deterministic workflows and language choice: Google ADK.** Python, TypeScript, Go, Java and Kotlin, graph workflows (ADK 2.0), and a skills toolset based on the open Agent Skills spec.
- **Best free way to start:** all three SDKs are free open-source packages. The model is the cost: Google's Gemini API has a free tier, while Claude and OpenAI models are paid API products.
- **Best fully open-source stack:** Google ADK (Apache-2.0 across Python, TypeScript, Go and Java repos) or the OpenAI Agents SDK (MIT). The Claude Agent SDK's Python repo is MIT, but Anthropic's docs say use is governed by its Commercial Terms.
- **Best if you do not want to be tied to one model vendor:** Vercel AI SDK, Mastra or LangGraph (see the alternatives section). We confirmed provider standardization for the AI SDK and `provider/model` naming for Mastra; check LangGraph and others for your target model.
- **Best for pure TypeScript web apps:** Vercel AI SDK or Mastra.

{% callout type="info" %}
Version check, November 27, 2026: the latest stable `openai-agents` on PyPI is **0.26.0** (uploaded November 24). The "1.0 alpha" in some write-ups is real but separate: GitHub has a pre-release **v1.0.0-alpha.2** (November 23) that is a GitHub-only preview bundling `openai-agents` with a native Codex runtime. It is not on PyPI. The Claude Agent SDK is at **0.2.189** (PyPI, November 25) and **0.3.332** (npm, November 24); the two registries use separate version lines. Google ADK is at **2.15.1** (PyPI, November 25).
{% /callout %}

