## How these SDKs differ in one paragraph each

**Claude Agent SDK** is a wrapper around a running agent: it launches the Claude Code CLI as a subprocess and gives you a typed message stream. You do not write the loop, you configure it (tools, permissions, hooks, subagents, MCP servers, skills). **OpenAI Agents SDK** gives you the loop as a library: an `Agent`, a `Runner`, and composable pieces you wire yourself, with the new `SandboxAgent` for file-heavy work. **Google ADK** gives you an agent runtime and a workflow engine: in 2.0, agents, tools and plain functions are nodes in a graph, and a CLI (`adk create`, `adk run`, `adk web`) scaffolds and tests projects.

{% figure src="https://adk.dev/assets/adk-graphs-fp.png" alt="Image from the ADK documentation on graph-based workflows" caption="ADK 2.0 treats agents, tools and functions as nodes in a graph. Source: Google (adk.dev)" /%}

## Claude Agent SDK

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/claude-agent-sdk-vs-openai-agents-sdk-vs-google-adk-2026/logos/claude-agent-sdk.png" name="Claude Agent SDK" size=40 /%}

### What it is

The Claude Agent SDK is Anthropic's library for building agents on top of the Claude Code runtime. Anthropic's overview describes it as building production agents "with Claude Code as a library". It was renamed from the Claude Code SDK on September 29, 2025, and Anthropic's Alex Albert wrote at the time that it was meant for "any general purpose agent, not just coding agents".

{% x id="1972718342197981194" /%}

The Python package (`claude-agent-sdk`) and the TypeScript package (`@anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk`) both expose a `query()` function that streams messages while Claude plans, calls tools and edits files. Because it drives Claude Code, you get Claude Code's built-in tools (Read, Edit, Glob, Grep, Bash and more), its permission modes, hooks, subagents and its `.claude/` conventions without writing them yourself.

{% x id="1972758349944111445" /%}

### Who it is for

Teams that want an agent that works on files and code today: internal coding agents, code review bots, document processing agents, and "Claude Code but inside my product" features (these are our suggested uses, not a vendor list). Apple's Xcode shipped a direct integration with the SDK in February 2026, which is the clearest first-party example.

{% x id="2018771170938724682" /%}

{% figure src="https://assets.claude.com/6d4a0d28992ade92d6fa63646fd9c9d318245c6c.jpg" alt="Social card image from Anthropic's announcement of the Xcode integration with the Claude Agent SDK" caption="Image Anthropic uses for its Xcode and Claude Agent SDK announcement. Source: Anthropic (anthropic.com)" /%}

### Key features

- **Built-in tools and permissions.** `allowed_tools` auto-approves tools; `permission_mode` supports default, `acceptEdits`, `bypassPermissions` and `plan` modes. Deny rules and ask rules from settings files apply even in bypass mode, per the permissions docs.
- **Hooks.** `PreToolUse`, `PostToolUse` and other events let you block or rewrite tool calls in code (the docs example protects `.env` files with a `PreToolUse` hook on `Write|Edit`).
- **Subagents.** Define them inline with `AgentDefinition` (description, prompt, tools, model) or via filesystem agents.
- **MCP.** Pass `mcp_servers` for external servers, or build in-process tools with `@tool` and `create_sdk_mcp_server` (TypeScript: `tool()` and `createSdkMcpServer`).
- **Sessions.** Continue, resume by ID and fork a session; `ClaudeSDKClient` (Python) or `continue: true` (TypeScript) handles multi-turn chat in one process.
- **Skills and project memory.** The SDK loads `CLAUDE.md`, `.claude/skills/`, agents and commands from the filesystem through `setting_sources` / `settingSources`.
- **Observability.** Traces, metrics and log events export over OpenTelemetry (OTLP) to any compatible backend.
- **Model providers.** Set `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, or use `CLAUDE_CODE_USE_BEDROCK=1`, `CLAUDE_CODE_USE_VERTEX=1` or `CLAUDE_CODE_USE_FOUNDRY=1` with the matching cloud credentials.

### Skills support

This is the most mature skills story of the three, because skills were a Claude Code concept first. From Anthropic's skills page: skills are filesystem artifacts (`.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`), discovered at startup from user and project directories, loaded fully only when Claude invokes them, and also invocable by you with `/<name>` in a prompt. You scope them with the `skills` option: a list of names, `"all"`, or `[]` for none. One important limit: **there is no programmatic API to register a skill**. Unlike subagents (the `agents` option), skills must exist as files on disk, or arrive through the `plugins` option.

```python
import asyncio
import os
from claude_agent_sdk import query, ClaudeAgentOptions

async def main():
    options = ClaudeAgentOptions(
        cwd=os.getcwd(),                      # .claude/skills/ here or in a parent
        setting_sources=["user", "project"],  # load skills from the filesystem
        skills="all",                         # let Claude invoke every discovered skill
        allowed_tools=["Read", "Write", "Bash"],
    )
    async for message in query(prompt="Help me process this PDF document", options=options):
        print(message)

asyncio.run(main())
```

*Adapted from Anthropic's "Extend agents with skills" docs page, fetched November 27, 2026.*

Skills in the [agentskills.codes catalog](https://agentskills.codes/skills/mcp-builder) use the same `SKILL.md` folder format that Claude Code reads, so a skill folder placed in `.claude/skills/` should be discoverable by an SDK agent. We did not test any specific catalog skill this way.

### Setup

```bash
pip install claude-agent-sdk      # or: npm install @anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk
export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=your-api-key
```

```python
import asyncio
from claude_agent_sdk import query, ClaudeAgentOptions, AssistantMessage, ResultMessage

async def main():
    async for message in query(
        prompt="Review utils.py for bugs that would cause crashes. Fix any issues you find.",
        options=ClaudeAgentOptions(
            allowed_tools=["Read", "Edit", "Glob"],  # auto-approve these tools
            permission_mode="acceptEdits",           # auto-approve file edits
        ),
    ):
        if isinstance(message, AssistantMessage):
            for block in message.content:
                if hasattr(block, "text"):
                    print(block.text)
        elif isinstance(message, ResultMessage):
            print(f"Done: {message.subtype}")

asyncio.run(main())
```

*Adapted from Anthropic's Agent SDK quickstart, fetched November 27, 2026.*

A subagent definition from the docs shows how focused roles are declared:

```python
from claude_agent_sdk import query, ClaudeAgentOptions, AgentDefinition

options = ClaudeAgentOptions(
    allowed_tools=["Read", "Grep", "Glob", "Agent", "Bash(npm test *)"],
    agents={
        "code-reviewer": AgentDefinition(
            description="Expert code review specialist. Use for quality, security, and maintainability reviews.",
            prompt="You are a code review specialist with expertise in security, performance, and best practices.",
            tools=["Read", "Grep", "Glob"],   # read-only
            model="sonnet",
        ),
    },
)
```

### Pros

- Production-tested loop and tools: you inherit Claude Code's behavior instead of rebuilding it.
- Best-in-class skills, hooks and permission controls, documented in one place.
- Subagents with per-agent tool allowlists and model overrides.
- OpenTelemetry export means traces land in whatever backend you already run.
- Runs against Bedrock, Vertex and Microsoft Foundry as well as Anthropic's API.
- Fast release cadence: 0.2.188 (November 23) and 0.2.189 (November 25).

### Cons

- Claude models only, as far as the docs we read show; we found no documented path to non-Claude models.
- It spawns and supervises a `claude` CLI subprocess, so hosting is heavier than a pure library: Anthropic's hosting guide spends pages on containers, session persistence and multi-tenant isolation.
- Anthropic's hosting guide lists "state that lives on local disk" and "ephemeral sessions" as hosting concerns: the CLI subprocess owns a working directory and session files, so you must plan persistence yourself.
- No in-SDK sandbox class; isolation is your job (Docker, Kubernetes, gVisor or a sandbox provider), or you move to Anthropic's separate Managed Agents product.
- Python and TypeScript only.
- Anthropic does not allow third parties to offer claude.ai login or rate limits in products built on the SDK, unless previously approved; you must use API-key style authentication.

### Benefits for your use case

If your agent mostly reads, edits and runs code or documents, you save weeks: file tools, shell, diff-style edits, permission prompts and context management already exist and are used daily by Claude Code users. If you already maintain `CLAUDE.md` and `.claude/skills/` for your human team, the SDK reuses them, so your agent and your developers follow the same instructions.

### Pricing and limits

The SDK is free. You pay Claude API rates, which (as of November 27, 2026, from Anthropic's pricing page) include Claude Opus 5.5 at $4 input / $20 output per MTok, Claude Sonnet 5.5 at $2 / $10, and Claude Fable 5.2 at $8 / $40. Prompt cache hits are billed at a fraction of base input (for example $0.10 per MTok on Sonnet 5.5). The hosting docs show a 20-turn cap (`max_turns=20`) as an example; you set the limit yourself.

### Standout use case

Building a coding or review agent that you can also hand to humans: the same `.claude/` folder drives both. The AI Engineer workshop below is a longer walkthrough.

{% youtube id="TqC1qOfiVcQ" title="Claude Agent SDK [Full Workshop] with Thariq Shihipar, Anthropic" /%}

*Conference video from the AI Engineer channel, confirmed to exist via YouTube oEmbed on November 27, 2026. We did not watch it or check its upload date; prefer the docs for current APIs.*

### Skip it if

You need a non-Claude model, need a tiny dependency footprint, or cannot run a CLI subprocess in your deployment target (for example a strict edge runtime). Also skip it if you want a deterministic graph of steps rather than a model-driven loop; ADK 2.0 or LangGraph fit that better.

## OpenAI Agents SDK

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/claude-agent-sdk-vs-openai-agents-sdk-vs-google-adk-2026/logos/openai-agents-sdk.png" name="OpenAI Agents SDK" size=40 /%}

### What it is

The OpenAI Agents SDK is a lightweight, open-source (MIT) framework for building multi-agent workflows. Its core is `Agent` (instructions, tools, handoffs, guardrails) plus `Runner`, which manages turns. The docs recommend the Responses API model class and also provide a Chat Completions model class; we did not verify how well non-OpenAI providers are supported.

On April 15, 2026, OpenAI announced "the next evolution of the Agents SDK": a more capable harness (configurable memory, sandbox-aware orchestration, Codex-like filesystem tools) and **native sandbox execution**, with built-in support for Blaxel, Cloudflare, Daytona, E2B, Modal, Runloop and Vercel, or bring your own.

{% x id="2044466699785920937" /%}

### Who it is for

Teams that want to assemble agent workflows from small parts, want hosted tracing without extra setup, and mostly use OpenAI models. The sandbox layer targets agents that inspect files, run commands and edit code, the same workload the Claude Agent SDK targets.

{% figure src="https://openai.github.io/openai-agents-python/assets/images/harness_with_compute.png" alt="Diagram from the OpenAI Agents SDK documentation about the harness and sandbox compute" caption="Image from the OpenAI Agents SDK docs (file name: harness_with_compute.png). Source: OpenAI (openai.github.io/openai-agents-python)" /%}

### Key features

- **Agents, handoffs and agents-as-tools.** A triage agent can hand off to specialists, or a manager agent can call specialists through `Agent.as_tool()` and keep control of the final answer. Handoffs appear to the model as `transfer_to_<agent_name>` tools by default.
- **Guardrails.** Input and output checks that can use a cheap, fast model and stop a run early.
- **Sessions.** Built-in implementations include `SQLiteSession`, `RedisSession`, `SQLAlchemySession`, `MongoDBSession`, `DaprSession`, `EncryptedSession`, `AdvancedSQLiteSession` and `OpenAIConversationsSession`, so history can be stored where you already run data.
- **Tracing.** On by default, with a Traces dashboard; disable with `OPENAI_AGENTS_DISABLE_TRACING=1` or in code.
- **Human in the loop.** Tools declare when they need approval; pending approvals appear as run interruptions, and `RunState` can be serialized so you can resume after a person decides, including for nested `Agent.as_tool()` calls.
- **MCP.** `MCPServerStdio`, `MCPServerStreamableHttp`, `MCPServerSse` and the hosted `HostedMCPTool`.
- **Hosted tools.** Web search, file search, shell, apply patch, computer use, code interpreter, image generation, and hosted tool search for deferring tool loading (Responses models only).
- **Sandbox agents.** `SandboxAgent` plus a `Manifest` (files, repos, mounts), capabilities (Shell, Filesystem, Skills, Memory, Compaction) and `SandboxRunConfig` to choose where work runs.

### Skills support

OpenAI's launch post lists "progressive disclosure via skills" among the primitives the harness integrates, and the docs give two concrete routes:

1. **Sandbox `Skills` capability.** Mount a local skills directory with `LocalDirLazySkillSource`; requested skills are copied into the sandbox workspace on demand. The docs say to prefer this over manually mounting `.agents/skills`.
2. **Hosted container shell skills.** `ShellTool` with `container_auto` can take `skills` entries: references to skills registered with OpenAI (a `skill_id` and `version`) or inline skill bundles, run in an OpenAI-managed container.

```python
from agents import Runner
from agents.run import RunConfig
from agents.sandbox import Manifest, SandboxAgent, SandboxRunConfig
from agents.sandbox.capabilities import Capabilities, LocalDirLazySkillSource, Skills
from agents.sandbox.entries import LocalDir
from agents.sandbox.sandboxes.unix_local import UnixLocalSandboxClient

agent = SandboxAgent(
    name="Sandbox engineer",
    model="<your model id>",   # the docs example passes a model name string here
    instructions="Read repo/task.md before editing files. Mention the verification command you ran.",
    default_manifest=Manifest(entries={"repo": LocalDir(src="./repo")}),
    capabilities=Capabilities.default() + [
        Skills(lazy_from=LocalDirLazySkillSource(source=LocalDir(src="./skills"))),
    ],
)

result = await Runner.run(
    agent,
    "Open repo/task.md, fix the issue, run the targeted test, and summarize the change.",
    run_config=RunConfig(sandbox=SandboxRunConfig(client=UnixLocalSandboxClient())),
)
print(result.final_output)
```

*Adapted from the OpenAI Agents SDK "Sandbox agents" docs page, fetched November 27, 2026. We replaced the model string with a placeholder.*

So yes, the OpenAI Agents SDK has skills support, but it is tied to the sandbox or hosted-shell paths rather than being a general-purpose `skills=[...]` option on every agent. If you run a plain `Agent` with function tools, there is no skill loader; you would add one yourself. For deeper coverage of this layer, see our sister site's [OpenAI Agents SDK sandbox, memory and long-horizon coding guide](https://agentpedia.codes/blog/openai-agents-sdk-sandbox-memory-coding-guide).

### Setup

```bash
pip install openai-agents      # or: uv add openai-agents
export OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...
```

```python
from agents import Agent
from agents.decorators import tool

@tool
def get_weather(city: str) -> str:
    """returns weather info for the specified city."""
    return f"The weather in {city} is sunny"

agent = Agent(
    name="Haiku agent",
    instructions="Always respond in haiku form",
    model="gpt-5-nano",
    tools=[get_weather],
)  # run with: result = await Runner.run(agent, "Weather in Paris?")
```

*Adapted from the OpenAI Agents SDK "Agents" docs page, fetched November 27, 2026. The `Runner.run` call is from the same page's examples; we did not execute this.*

A handoff setup, from the handoffs docs page:

```python
from agents import Agent, handoff

billing_agent = Agent(name="Billing agent")
refund_agent = Agent(name="Refund agent")
triage_agent = Agent(name="Triage agent", handoffs=[billing_agent, handoff(refund_agent)])
```

And an MCP server over stdio (adapted from the MCP docs page):

```python
from agents import Agent, Runner
from agents.mcp import MCPServerStdio

async with MCPServerStdio(
    name="Filesystem Server via npx",
    params={"command": "npx", "args": ["-y", "@modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem", "./sample_files"]},
    client_session_timeout_seconds=30,
) as server:
    agent = Agent(
        name="Assistant",
        instructions="Use the files in the sample directory to answer questions.",
        mcp_servers=[server],
    )
    result = await Runner.run(agent, "List the files available to you.")
    print(result.final_output)
```

### Pros

- Small, readable surface: `Agent`, `Runner`, tools, handoffs. Easy to reason about and test.
- Tracing is on by default with a dashboard, and custom trace processors can push traces to other destinations.
- Broadest documented session backends (SQLite, Redis, SQLAlchemy, MongoDB, Dapr, encrypted, OpenAI-managed).
- Sandbox layer with many providers (Blaxel, Cloudflare, Daytona, E2B, Modal, Runloop, Vercel, Docker, Databricks) and a documented harness/compute split that, per the launch post, helps keep credentials out of environments where model-generated code runs.
- Human-in-the-loop approvals with serializable `RunState`, so paused runs can be resumed after a decision.
- MIT license; Python and TypeScript SDKs.

### Cons

- Pre-1.0. Stable is 0.26.0, with a 1.0 alpha on GitHub only; OpenAI's release policy says minor versions may break non-beta interfaces, and the 0.22.0 notes say that release tightens failure handling and data isolation for several existing APIs.
- Some of the best features are tied to OpenAI's Responses API (hosted tool search, programmatic tool calling, hosted containers). Hosted tool search, for example, is documented as available only with OpenAI Responses models.
- Skills are scoped to sandbox and hosted shell flows, not a general agent option.
- The unix-local sandbox adds no OS-level confinement on Linux; for untrusted input the docs tell you to use Docker, a hosted sandbox or external isolation.
- Sandbox providers bill separately, and we did not verify their prices.
- Sandbox agents launched Python-first: the April 15 launch post says TypeScript support is planned for a later release. We did not verify whether the TypeScript SDK has caught up since, so check before committing.

### Benefits for your use case

You get a framework with a small surface: write agents as data, compose them with handoffs, and ship. If you want to experiment with sandboxes, you can start with the local Unix client and move the same `SandboxAgent` to a hosted provider by changing the client in `SandboxRunConfig`, which is the portability pitch of the `Manifest` abstraction. We did not run this migration ourselves.

{% x id="2044479379452137655" /%}

### Pricing and limits

The SDK is MIT and free. The April announcement says the new sandbox and harness capabilities use standard API pricing "based on tokens and tool use". As of November 27, 2026, OpenAI's pricing page lists, per MTok, gpt-6.2-sol at $2.00 input / $10.00 output (cached input $0.10), gpt-6.2-astra at $8.00 / $40.00, gpt-6-astra at $10.00 / $50.00 and gpt-6.1-sol at $2.00 / $10.00 on standard processing, with Batch at half those rates. Tracing storage and any sandbox provider fees are separate lines we did not price.

### Standout use case

A multi-agent support or research workflow where you want handoffs, guardrails and tracing from day one, or a Codex-like file-editing agent where you want to choose your own sandbox vendor.

### Skip it if

You need deterministic multi-step graphs with checkpointing semantics (look at ADK 2.0 or LangGraph), you want a turnkey coding agent with a rich permission model (Claude Agent SDK), or you cannot accept a pre-1.0 API.

## Google ADK (Agent Development Kit)

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/claude-agent-sdk-vs-openai-agents-sdk-vs-google-adk-2026/logos/google-adk.png" name="Google ADK" size=40 /%}

### What it is

ADK is Google's open-source (Apache-2.0) framework for building, evaluating and deploying agents. It is optimized for Gemini and Google Cloud but its models page documents Claude models, LiteLLM, Ollama, vLLM and Apigee. Python, TypeScript, Go, Java and Kotlin are all supported, though feature parity varies by language and page.

**ADK 2.0** is the biggest change: it introduces a Workflow Runtime that moves ADK "from a hierarchical agent executor to a graph-based execution engine", where agents, tools and functions run as nodes. Per the ADK docs, ADK Python 2.0 reached general availability on May 19, 2026, Go 2.0 on June 30, 2026 and TypeScript 2.0 on August 21, 2026. The docs also list breaking changes from 1.x, including a new `Event` schema (`node_info`, `output`) that affects custom session storage.

{% x id="2072063955607192057" /%}

### Who it is for

Teams that want explicit control over agent steps (graph workflows with retries and human input nodes), need Java, Go or Kotlin, or deploy on Google Cloud (Agent Runtime on Agent Platform, Cloud Run, GKE) and want Cloud Trace.

{% figure src="https://adk.dev/assets/hp-adk-web1.png" alt="Image from the ADK documentation homepage showing the ADK web interface" caption="The ADK dev UI launched with `adk web`. Source: Google (adk.dev)" /%}

### Key features

- **Workflows (2.0).** `Workflow(name=..., edges=[("START", agent_a, function_b, agent_c)])` with each node's return value passed to the next. Dynamic workflows use plain code for loops and branching; collaborative workflows use coordinator agents with sub-agents.
- **Human input nodes.** A node that `yield`s `RequestInput(message=...)` pauses the workflow until the user replies, with no model call needed, so the pause is deterministic. Google's Go 2.0 announcement also lists native human-in-the-loop primitives and built-in retry.
- **Skills.** `SkillToolset` with `load_skill_from_dir` (below).
- **MCP.** `McpToolset` with `StdioConnectionParams` and an optional `tool_filter`.
- **Sessions and memory.** `SessionService` abstractions and `MemoryService` options: `InMemoryMemoryService`, `VertexAiMemoryBankService` and `VertexAiRagMemoryService`.
- **Code execution.** `BuiltInCodeExecutor` (Gemini's code execution tool, with a documented single-tool-per-agent limitation), `ExecuteBashTool` (local workspace, POSIX only, Python 1.27.0+) and a Daytona plugin for isolated sandboxes.
- **Observability.** The ADK docs describe trace views for debugging, OpenTelemetry configuration, a Google Cloud Trace integration page and a Langfuse integration page.
- **Evaluation.** A built-in evaluation framework (Python) for scoring trajectories and final answers.
- **A2A.** Agent2Agent protocol support (Python, Go and Java; experimental in Go and Java).
- **Deployment.** The deploy page features Agent Runtime on Google's Agent Platform and Cloud Run.

### Skills support

ADK is the only one of the three whose skills page covers several languages at once: Python (v1.25.0+), TypeScript (v0.6.1+), Go (v1.2.0+) and Kotlin (v0.1.0+), with the feature marked experimental in TypeScript and Kotlin. It is based on the Agent Skills specification and loads skills incrementally to protect the context window. You can define skills in code or load them from a directory.

```python
import pathlib
from google.adk import Agent
from google.adk.skills import load_skill_from_dir
from google.adk.tools import skill_toolset

weather_skill = load_skill_from_dir(
    pathlib.Path(__file__).parent / "skills" / "weather-skill"
)

def get_weather_tool(city: str) -> dict:
    """Retrieves the current weather report for a specified city."""
    return {"status": "success", "report": f"The weather in {city} is sunny with a temperature of 25C."}

my_skill_toolset = skill_toolset.SkillToolset(
    skills=[weather_skill],
    additional_tools=[get_weather_tool],
)

root_agent = Agent(
    model="gemini-flash-latest",
    name="skill_user_agent",
    description="An agent that can use specialized skills.",
    instruction="You are a helpful assistant that can leverage skills to perform tasks.",
    tools=[my_skill_toolset],
)
```

*Adapted from the ADK "Skills for ADK agents" page, fetched November 27, 2026.*

The design differs from Claude's: in ADK a skill is a toolset object you attach to an agent, and the page says skills can be defined in code or loaded from a filesystem. That is explicit, though you wire it yourself rather than getting an ambient `.claude/skills/` convention.

### Setup

```bash
pip install google-adk
adk create my_agent    # scaffold a project
adk run my_agent       # terminal chat
adk web --port 8000    # dev UI with trace view
```

A graph workflow from the ADK 2.0 docs:

```python
from google.adk import Agent, Workflow, Event
from pydantic import BaseModel

city_generator_agent = Agent(
    name="city_generator_agent",
    model="gemini-flash-latest",
    instruction="Return the name of a random city. Return only the name, nothing else.",
)

class CityTime(BaseModel):
    time_info: str
    city: str

def lookup_time_function(node_input: str):
    """Simulate returning the current time in the specified city."""
    return CityTime(time_info="10:10 AM", city=node_input)

city_report_agent = Agent(
    name="city_report_agent",
    model="gemini-flash-latest",
    input_schema=CityTime,
    instruction="You receive a CityTime. Output one line stating that it is that time in that city.",
)

def completed_message_function(node_input: str):
    return Event(message=f"{node_input}\n WORKFLOW COMPLETED.")

root_agent = Workflow(
    name="root_agent",
    edges=[("START", city_generator_agent, lookup_time_function,
            city_report_agent, completed_message_function)],
)
```

*Condensed from the ADK 2.0 "Graph-based workflows" page, fetched November 27, 2026.*

And an MCP filesystem server:

```python
from google.adk.agents import LlmAgent
from google.adk.tools.mcp_tool import McpToolset
from google.adk.tools.mcp_tool.mcp_session_manager import StdioConnectionParams
from mcp import StdioServerParameters

root_agent = LlmAgent(
    model="gemini-flash-latest",
    name="filesystem_assistant",
    instruction="Help users manage local files.",
    tools=[McpToolset(
        connection_params=StdioConnectionParams(
            server_params=StdioServerParameters(
                command="npx",
                args=["-y", "@modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem", "/abs/path/accessible_files"],
            ),
        ),
        tool_filter=["list_directory", "read_file"],  # optional
    )],
)
```

### Pros

- Five languages documented, Apache-2.0 on the repos we checked, with Python, Go and TypeScript all at 2.0 GA per the docs.
- Graph workflows with deterministic human-input nodes: the clearest "reliable multi-step" story of the three.
- Skills toolset documented across Python, TypeScript, Go and Kotlin.
- Documented memory options (in-memory, Memory Bank, RAG-backed) and Google Cloud deployment paths.
- Dev UI (`adk web`), built-in evaluation framework (Python) and Cloud Trace integration.
- The docs say 2.0 is designed to be compatible with ADK 1.x agents, with a documented list of breaking changes.

### Cons

- The center of gravity is Gemini and Google Cloud. Other models are supported, but docs, memory services and deploy options lean on Google products.
- 2.0 introduced real migration work (event schema, session storage) for existing 1.x apps.
- Feature parity differs by language: skills are experimental in TypeScript and Kotlin, `ExecuteBashTool` is Python-only, A2A is experimental in Go and Java, evaluation is documented for Python.
- Code execution is thinner than the other two: the built-in executor is limited to one tool per agent, the bash tool is local and POSIX-only, and isolated sandboxes come through a Daytona plugin rather than a first-party sandbox abstraction.
- Python 3.11 or newer is required for the PyPI package.
- We did not check pricing for Google Cloud deployment targets.

### Benefits for your use case

When the process matters as much as the output (approvals, retries, data validation between steps), ADK 2.0 lets you encode the process as a graph and keep the model for the steps that need judgment. If your team is not all Python, ADK is the only one of the three with Java and Go SDKs (we did not compare their maturity).

{% youtube id="fOIGMZusCs8" title="Google ADK 2.0 workflows tutorial: Building reliable multi-agent systems" /%}

*Video from the Google Cloud Tech channel, confirmed to exist via YouTube oEmbed on November 27, 2026. We did not watch it or check its upload date.*

### Pricing and limits

ADK is free. Models: as of November 27, 2026, Google's Gemini API pricing page lists Gemini 4 Hydrogen at $1.50 input / $7.50 output per MTok, Gemini 4 Neon at $3.00 / $15.00, and Gemini 4 Carbon at $2.00 / $10.00 through November 30, 2026, rising to $4.00 / $20.00 starting December 1, 2026 (Carbon is not on the Free tier). Gemini 3.8 Flash is $0.75 / $3.75 through December 31, 2026, then $1.50 / $7.50 from January 1, 2027. The Gemini API has a Free tier with usage limits and a Paid tier with context caching and a 50% Batch API discount.

### Standout use case

A back-office or data pipeline agent where steps must run in a fixed order with a human approval in the middle, deployable to Cloud Run or Agent Runtime.

### Skip it if

You want a ready-made coding agent (Claude Agent SDK), you want the smallest possible API (OpenAI Agents SDK), or you are unwilling to adopt a graph model and 2.0 migration notes.

## Other alternatives worth considering

These five are shorter sections. They are here because each one wins a case the big three do not: provider flexibility, TypeScript-first web apps, graph durability, or Azure alignment. We read each project's own documentation on November 27, 2026 and did not run any of them.

### LangGraph

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/claude-agent-sdk-vs-openai-agents-sdk-vs-google-adk-2026/logos/langgraph.png" name="LangGraph" size=40 /%}

LangGraph's docs describe it as "a low-level orchestration framework and runtime for long-running, stateful agents": you define workflows as graphs of shared state, nodes and edges, and mix deterministic and LLM-driven steps. The overview says it focuses on orchestration capabilities such as durable execution, streaming and human-in-the-loop, and that it does not prescribe prompts or agent architecture. Python (`langgraph` 1.2.24) and JavaScript (`@langchain/langgraph` 1.7.5) are both MIT-licensed; the Python repo had 45,512 stars on November 27, 2026, the most of any project in this post.

- **Pros:** durable execution and human-in-the-loop are first-class; the biggest community of the group by GitHub stars.
- **Cons:** low-level by design, so you write more yourself; observability is typically through LangSmith, which is a separate product we did not price; no built-in coding tools.
- **Skip it if:** you want a ready-made file and shell agent, or you just need two agents and a handoff.

### Vercel AI SDK

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/claude-agent-sdk-vs-openai-agents-sdk-vs-google-adk-2026/logos/vercel-ai-sdk.png" name="Vercel AI SDK" size=40 /%}

The AI SDK (`ai` 7.0.183, published November 26, 2026) is a TypeScript toolkit that standardizes model providers and ships UI primitives for React, Next.js, Vue, Svelte and Node. Its introduction page also mentions a harness abstraction, `HarnessAgent`, for using established harnesses such as Claude Code, Codex and Pi behind the same stream primitives. That is a notable design choice: instead of competing with the Claude Agent SDK, it can sit on top of coding-agent harnesses.

- **Pros:** best fit when your agent lives inside a web app; provider-neutral; frequent releases.
- **Cons:** TypeScript only; not a sandbox or skills runtime by itself in the pages we read.
- **Skip it if:** your stack is Python or Java, or your agent is a backend worker with no UI.

### Mastra

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/claude-agent-sdk-vs-openai-agents-sdk-vs-google-adk-2026/logos/mastra.png" name="Mastra" size=40 /%}

Mastra describes itself as "a TypeScript framework for building AI agents and applications" (`@mastra/core` 1.86.0, November 25, 2026). Its quickstart opens Mastra Studio locally (port 4111) for building and testing agents, workflows and tools, and it offers a general-purpose agent harness with a local workspace, shell tools, memory, task tracking, web access and recurring schedules. Its docs even tell local coding agents to install Mastra's own skills with `npx skills add mastra-ai/skills`, which is a neat example of the skills ecosystem reaching framework docs. Models are named as `provider/model` strings.

- **Pros:** TypeScript-native, with a visual Studio and a harness that includes memory, task tracking and schedules.
- **Cons:** TypeScript only; license shows as Apache-2.0 on npm but GitHub's API reported NOASSERTION, so read the repo's license file before you rely on it.
- **Skip it if:** you are not a TypeScript shop.

### Microsoft Agent Framework

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/claude-agent-sdk-vs-openai-agents-sdk-vs-google-adk-2026/logos/microsoft-agent-framework.png" name="Microsoft Agent Framework" size=40 /%}

Microsoft's overview lists four areas: Agents (supporting Microsoft Foundry, Anthropic, Azure OpenAI, OpenAI, Ollama and more, with MCP clients and middleware), a **Harness Agent** for long multi-step tasks (planning and todo tracking, context compaction, file access and memory, "don't-ask-again" tool approval, observability), Workflows (functional and graph-based) and Integrations. The Python package `agent-framework` is at 1.26.0 (November 12, 2026) and labelled Production/Stable with an MIT classifier. We could not retrieve GitHub star counts for the main repository because the API returned 404 for `microsoft/agent-framework` when we tried, so we leave that number out.

- **Pros:** multi-provider including Anthropic; workflows plus a harness; a natural fit for Azure shops.
- **Cons:** docs are Microsoft Learn pages, and the page we opened showed an authorization notice in places, so some content may need sign-in; we did not assess the API size.
- **Skip it if:** you are not on Azure or .NET and want something smaller.

### Strands Agents

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/claude-agent-sdk-vs-openai-agents-sdk-vs-google-adk-2026/logos/strands-agents.png" name="Strands Agents" size=40 /%}

Strands Agents (`strands-agents` 1.67.0 on PyPI, November 25, 2026; `@strands-agents/sdk` 1.26.0 on npm) is described on PyPI as "a model-driven approach to building AI agents in just a few lines of code", under Apache-2.0, with Python and TypeScript packages. The project page now leads with a "Strands harness": an agent with tools, memory, session management and context management already configured (`pip install strands-harness`, `npm install @strands-agents/harness`). The star count we report (9,300 on November 27, 2026) is from `strands-agents/harness-sdk`.

- **Pros:** Apache-2.0, Python and TypeScript, and a batteries-included harness with memory, sessions and context management.
- **Cons:** smaller star count than LangGraph; the packaging now spans SDK and harness packages, so check which one the tutorial you follow uses.
- **Skip it if:** you need Java or Go, or already standardized on one of the big three.

{% figure src="https://strandsagents.com/og-image.png" alt="Strands Agents site social card" caption="Strands Agents' site social image. Source: Strands Agents (strandsagents.com)" /%}

