## Feature matrix

"Yes" means the vendor's docs describe a first-party feature. "Partial" means a narrower or plugin-based version exists. "Not verified" means we did not confirm it in the pages we read.

| Feature | Claude Agent SDK | OpenAI Agents SDK | Google ADK |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Languages | Yes: Python, TypeScript | Yes: Python, TypeScript | Yes: Python, TypeScript, Go, Java, Kotlin |
| Skills support (SKILL.md) | Yes: filesystem skills, `skills` option, `/name` dispatch; no programmatic registration | Partial: sandbox `Skills` capability and hosted shell skills; not a general agent option | Yes: `SkillToolset`, defined in code or loaded from a directory; experimental in TypeScript and Kotlin |
| Sandbox | Partial: hosting and secure-deployment guidance for containers and sandbox providers; no in-SDK provider layer found | Yes: `SandboxAgent` with Unix-local, Docker and hosted providers | Partial: Daytona plugin, local bash tool, Gemini code execution |
| MCP | Yes: external servers plus in-process SDK servers | Yes: stdio, Streamable HTTP, SSE, hosted MCP | Yes: `McpToolset` with stdio and tool filter |
| Tracing | Yes: OpenTelemetry export | Yes: default Traces dashboard, custom processors, disable via env var | Yes: trace views, Cloud Trace, Langfuse, OTel (per ADK docs) |
| Multi-agent | Yes: subagents (`AgentDefinition`) | Yes: handoffs and agents-as-tools | Yes: sub-agents, graph workflows, A2A |
| Memory | Partial: CLAUDE.md and project files; no managed long-term memory service found in docs read | Yes: sandbox Memory capability; sessions for history | Yes: `MemoryService` options (in-memory, Memory Bank, RAG; Memory Bank and RAG need `google-adk[gcp]`) |
| Sessions | Yes: continue, resume, fork | Yes: many backends (SQLite, Redis, SQLAlchemy, Mongo, Dapr, encrypted) | Yes: `SessionService` abstraction |
| Human in the loop | Yes: `can_use_tool` / `canUseTool` callback, permission modes, plan mode | Yes: approvals, interruptions, serializable `RunState` | Yes: `RequestInput` nodes; native human-in-the-loop primitives in Go 2.0 per Google |
| Non-vendor models | No documented path | Partial: Responses and Chat Completions model classes; some features Responses-only; other providers not verified | Partial: Claude, LiteLLM, Ollama, vLLM documented |
| Evaluation tooling | Not verified | Not verified | Yes: built-in evaluation framework (Python) |

A compact view of the alternatives, from their own docs:

| Feature | LangGraph | Vercel AI SDK | Mastra | Microsoft Agent Framework | Strands Agents |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Languages | Python, JavaScript | TypeScript | TypeScript | Python (.NET not verified) | Python, TypeScript |
| Skills | Not verified | Harness abstraction for coding agents | Publishes skills for coding agents | Not verified | Not verified |
| Sandbox | Not verified | Not verified | Local workspace and shell | Not verified | Not verified |
| MCP | Not verified | Not verified | Not verified | Yes (MCP clients) | Not verified |
| Tracing | Via LangSmith (separate product) | Not verified | Studio | "Observability" in Harness Agent | Not verified |
| Multi-agent | Yes: graphs of nodes | Agent surfaces | Workflows | Yes: workflows | Not verified |
| Memory | Not verified | Not verified | Yes | Yes (Harness Agent) | Yes (harness) |
| Sessions | Yes: persistence | Not verified | Not verified | Not verified | Yes (harness) |
| Human in the loop | Yes: human-in-the-loop | Not verified | Not verified | Yes: tool approval | Not verified |

## Head-to-head: which to pick

### Claude Agent SDK vs OpenAI Agents SDK

**Pick the Claude Agent SDK** when the agent's main job is to work on a codebase or document tree and you want mature permissions, hooks and skills now. It brings Claude Code's behavior with it. **Pick the OpenAI Agents SDK** when you want a small orchestration core, default tracing, many session backends and a choice of sandbox vendors, and your agents will use OpenAI models (or you accept reduced features on others).

The honest split on skills: Claude's are ambient (drop folders in `.claude/skills/`), OpenAI's are attached to a sandbox or hosted shell. The honest split on sandboxes: OpenAI ships a provider abstraction in the SDK; with Claude you bring your own container and Anthropic's hosting guide tells you how.

### OpenAI Agents SDK vs Google ADK

**Pick the OpenAI Agents SDK** for the quickest path from idea to a working multi-agent app with traces. **Pick Google ADK** when you need explicit graph control and human-input nodes, Java or Go, or Google Cloud deployment. ADK's 2.0 workflow model is the main differentiator: OpenAI's handoffs let the model route, while ADK graph edges let you route. If you need both, ADK also supports A2A, so a mixed system is plausible, though we did not test cross-SDK interoperability.

### Claude Agent SDK vs Google ADK

**Pick the Claude Agent SDK** for a single powerful, model-driven worker. **Pick Google ADK** for a controlled pipeline with many roles, languages or Google services. On skills, ADK's explicit `SkillToolset` is wired in code, while Claude's filesystem approach is easier to share with human developers using Claude Code. We did not test either for testability.

### Claude Agent SDK vs LangGraph

This pairing shows up in search suggestions, and it is a category difference. The Claude Agent SDK is a **ready-made agent** that you configure; LangGraph is a **runtime for graphs** you design. If you want an agent that edits files tonight, use the SDK. If you want durable, long-running, stateful multi-step workflows that you design yourself, use LangGraph. Some teams may combine both: a LangGraph node that calls a Claude agent for the file-heavy step. We have not tested that combination, and we have no usage data on how common it is.

## Skills and MCP servers that work with these tools

These are real entries in the [agentskills.codes catalog](https://agentskills.codes/skills/mcp-builder). Each page returned HTTP 200 on November 27, 2026. They are community or vendor-published skills; we read the catalog descriptions but did not install or run them with the SDKs above, so treat "works with" as "targets the same technology" rather than a tested guarantee. Install counts are from the catalog API on the same date.

- [mcp-builder](https://agentskills.codes/skills/mcp-builder) (anthropics, 136 installs): a guide for building high-quality MCP servers. Every SDK in this post can consume MCP servers, so this is the most portable skill here.
- [mcp-integration](https://agentskills.codes/skills/mcp-integration) (anthropics, 21 installs): triggers on requests to add or configure MCP servers, useful when wiring `mcp_servers` into a Claude agent.
- [openai-docs](https://agentskills.codes/skills/openai-docs) (openai): for building with OpenAI products and APIs using up-to-date official docs, a good companion while you work with Agents SDK APIs that change often.
- [openai-knowledge](https://agentskills.codes/skills/openai-knowledge) (openai): guidance for the OpenAI API (Responses API) and platform features, which the Agents SDK builds on.
- [adk-agent-builder](https://agentskills.codes/skills/adk-agent-builder) (jeremylongshore): describes building production agents with Google's Agent Development Kit.
- [adk-engineer](https://agentskills.codes/skills/adk-engineer) (jeremylongshore): ADK engineering best practices for production-ready agents.
- [adk-deploy-guide](https://agentskills.codes/skills/adk-deploy-guide) (kumaran-is): deployment guidance for ADK agents on Google Cloud (Cloud Run, Agent Engine, Vertex AI).
- [agent-evaluation](https://agentskills.codes/skills/agent-evaluation) (davila7): behavioral testing and benchmarking for LLM agents, relevant whichever SDK you choose.
- [langgraph](https://agentskills.codes/skills/langgraph) (davila7): a LangGraph expert skill for the stateful, multi-actor alternative.
- [langsmith-fetch](https://agentskills.codes/skills/langsmith-fetch) (ComposioHQ): fetches execution traces from LangSmith to debug LangChain and LangGraph agents.
- [phoenix-tracing](https://agentskills.codes/skills/phoenix-tracing) (Arize-ai): OpenInference conventions for Phoenix observability, relevant to the OpenTelemetry paths in Claude and ADK.

For deeper skill picks, see our posts on the [best Claude Code skills for developers](https://agentskills.codes/blog/best-claude-code-skills-for-developers-2026) and the [best Codex skills](https://agentskills.codes/blog/best-codex-skills-2026).

## Best for X: decision table

| If you need... | Pick | Why |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A coding or document agent with permissions and hooks, fast | Claude Agent SDK | Same tools, hooks and skills as Claude Code |
| Lightest multi-agent core with default tracing | OpenAI Agents SDK | `Agent` plus `Runner`, handoffs, guardrails, Traces |
| Hosted or pluggable sandboxes for file-heavy agents | OpenAI Agents SDK | `SandboxAgent` with Blaxel, Cloudflare, Daytona, E2B, Modal, Runloop, Vercel, Docker |
| Deterministic steps with human approval nodes | Google ADK | 2.0 graph workflows and `RequestInput` nodes |
| Java, Go or Kotlin | Google ADK | The only one of the three with those languages documented |
| Skills defined in code | Google ADK | `SkillToolset` supports in-code or filesystem skills |
| Reuse a team's existing `.claude/skills/` | Claude Agent SDK | Loaded through `setting_sources` |
| Durable, long-running graph execution | LangGraph | Stateful orchestration runtime with durable execution and human-in-the-loop |
| Agent inside a React or Next.js app | Vercel AI SDK or Mastra | TypeScript-first with UI or Studio |
| Azure and Foundry shop | Microsoft Agent Framework | Foundry, Azure OpenAI and Anthropic support |
| Apache-2.0 Python or TypeScript harness with memory and sessions preconfigured | Strands Agents | `strands-harness` and `@strands-agents/harness` packages |

## Common mistakes

1. **Choosing by GitHub stars.** On November 27, 2026 the OpenAI Python repo (30,902) beat ADK Python (22,378) and Claude's Python repo (8,657), but stars say nothing about fit; the Claude Agent SDK wraps the Claude Code CLI, so its repo count is not directly comparable.
2. **Assuming "1.0" exists.** `openai-agents` is 0.26.0 on PyPI. The v1.0.0-alpha.2 build is a GitHub-only preview with a native Codex runtime; do not put it in production dependencies.
3. **Using the local sandbox with untrusted input.** OpenAI's own docs say the Unix-local client has no OS-level confinement on Linux. Use Docker or a hosted sandbox.
4. **Expecting skills to be one API.** The three SDKs load skills in three different ways (filesystem convention, sandbox capability or hosted reference, toolset object). A skill folder may be reusable, but the wiring is not.
5. **Forgetting persistence.** Claude Agent SDK sessions are session files owned by the CLI subprocess on local disk, per the hosting guide; plan how to persist them if containers are replaced.
6. **Ignoring the vendor lock in model choice.** Claude's SDK docs describe only Claude models; OpenAI's best features need Responses models; ADK leans on Gemini. If model neutrality matters, weigh the AI SDK, Mastra or Microsoft Agent Framework.
7. **Skipping migration notes.** ADK 2.0 changes the Event schema, which matters if you built custom session storage on 1.x.

## Buying and setup checklist

- Write down the job in one sentence: file-heavy worker, orchestrated team, or deterministic pipeline.
- Pick the language(s) first; that alone narrows ADK versus the other two.
- Pin SDK versions (all three move weekly) and read release notes before upgrading.
- Decide where code runs: container, hosted sandbox or your laptop; do not use unconfined local execution for untrusted input.
- Choose a trace backend on day one (OpenAI Traces, OTLP for Claude, Cloud Trace or Langfuse for ADK).
- Decide how sessions persist (database, Redis, managed service).
- Test your skills folder in the target SDK before assuming portability.
- Estimate monthly model cost from your own token counts using the vendor price pages above.
- Plan for human approval on destructive tools (permission modes, `RunState` approvals or `RequestInput`).
- Put a turn or step cap on every run (the Claude hosting docs show `max_turns=20` as an example).

## How we evaluated

This is a documentation and registry comparison. On November 27, 2026 we:

- Queried PyPI and npm for current versions, upload dates and license fields of all eight SDKs.
- Queried the GitHub API for stars, license, push dates and release tags (the OpenAI releases list is where the v1.0.0-alpha tags appear).
- Fetched and read the official docs: Anthropic's Agent SDK pages (overview, quickstart, skills, MCP, subagents, sessions, permissions, hosting, observability, hooks), the OpenAI Agents SDK docs (release notes, sandbox guide and clients, tools, sessions, human in the loop, tracing, MCP, multi-agent, handoffs, guardrails) and the ADK docs on adk.dev (2.0, graphs, human input, skills, MCP, memory, sessions, observability, deploy, code execution).
- Fetched the three vendors' model pricing pages (Anthropic, OpenAI, Google).
- Verified YouTube videos through oEmbed, X posts through the public syndication endpoint, images through HTTP HEAD, and every internal link through HTTP status.
- Queried the agentskills.codes catalog API for matching skills.

We did **not** install or run any of the SDKs, did not run any of the linked skills, did not benchmark speed or quality, and did not price sandbox providers or Google Cloud deployment. We found no neutral head-to-head benchmark of these three SDKs, so none is reported. Code samples are adapted from vendor docs and not executed by us. Vendor claims are attributed as such.

## FAQ

### Is the OpenAI Agents SDK at version 1.0?

Not as a stable release. As of November 27, 2026 PyPI's latest is 0.26.0 (November 24). GitHub has pre-releases v1.0.0-alpha.1 (November 20) and v1.0.0-alpha.2 (November 23); the alpha.2 notes call it a GitHub-only preview bundling `openai-agents` with a native Codex runtime, and warn that new persisted state formats mean you should upgrade checkpoint readers before writers.

### Does the OpenAI Agents SDK support skills?

Yes, in two places. The sandbox `Skills` capability (for example with `LocalDirLazySkillSource`) copies requested skills into the sandbox workspace, and the hosted `ShellTool` container mode accepts skill references and inline skill bundles. A plain `Agent` with function tools has no built-in skills loader in the docs we read.

### Does the OpenAI Agents SDK have a sandbox?

Yes. `SandboxAgent` runs against a sandbox client chosen in `SandboxRunConfig`: Unix-local, Docker or a hosted provider. OpenAI's April 15, 2026 post names Blaxel, Cloudflare, Daytona, E2B, Modal, Runloop and Vercel. The docs warn that Unix-local has no OS-level confinement on Linux.

### What is the difference between the Claude Agent SDK and the Claude API?

The API gives you model calls and tool use; you write the loop. The Agent SDK gives you the Claude Code loop, built-in tools and permission system, which spawns a `claude` CLI subprocess. Anthropic also offers Managed Agents, where Anthropic hosts the loop; we did not evaluate it in depth.

### Can the Claude Agent SDK use non-Claude models?

We found no documented path in Anthropic's docs. It can use Claude models through Anthropic's API, Amazon Bedrock, Google Cloud's Agent Platform (Vertex) or Microsoft Foundry. For multi-provider agents consider the Vercel AI SDK, Mastra or Microsoft Agent Framework, which list several providers in their docs.

### Can I use my Claude subscription login in an app built on the Agent SDK?

Anthropic's docs say that, unless previously approved, third-party developers may not offer claude.ai login or rate limits for their products built on the SDK; use API key authentication per the quickstart. Check Anthropic's current terms for your exact case.

### Is Google ADK only for Gemini?

No. ADK is optimized for Gemini but documents Claude models, LiteLLM, Ollama and vLLM support, plus Apigee. Some features (Memory Bank, Agent Runtime deployment, code execution tool) are Google Cloud or Gemini-specific.

### What changed in ADK 2.0?

ADK 2.0 adds a graph-based Workflow Runtime, dynamic workflows in code and collaborative coordinator-and-subagent setups. Per the docs, Python 2.0 was GA on May 19, 2026, Go on June 30 and TypeScript on August 21. It lists breaking changes from 1.x, including Event schema fields that affect custom session storage.

### Which SDK has the best tracing?

It depends on where you want traces. OpenAI's is on by default with a hosted dashboard. Claude exports OpenTelemetry to any OTLP backend. ADK has a local web UI trace view, a Cloud Trace integration and a Langfuse integration. We did not benchmark overhead or usability.

### Which SDK is best for MCP?

All three support MCP well. OpenAI documents stdio, Streamable HTTP, SSE and hosted MCP; Claude supports external servers plus in-process servers created with `create_sdk_mcp_server`; ADK uses `McpToolset` with an optional tool filter. Pick on the rest of your stack.

### Can I reuse the same SKILL.md folder across all three?

Possibly for the folder itself: ADK says its skills follow the Agent Skills specification, and the Claude and OpenAI docs both describe `SKILL.md` folders. The loading code differs, and we did not test a single skill across all three SDKs, so validate in your target.

### Are these SDKs free?

The SDK packages are free under open-source or permissive terms (Claude's terms point to Anthropic's Commercial Terms). Model usage, sandbox providers and hosting are paid; see the pricing table above.

### Which should a beginner pick?

For a first agent on any stack, the OpenAI Agents SDK surface is the smallest (`Agent`, `Runner`, a decorated function). If you mainly want to automate work in a code repo, the Claude Agent SDK quickstart builds a bug-fixing agent in a few lines. If you are on Google Cloud, `adk create` scaffolds a project and `adk web` gives you a local UI.

